// A user struct takes a prelude name whose FIELDS a surviving prelude MODULE
// function reads.
//
// The shadow filter that builds the throwaway program dropped impls only, so the
// prelude's own `struct Arena` stayed in it beside the user's - two declarations
// of one name, in a program built to answer a question about that name. It
// answered nothing instead: unable to say which `Arena` it meant, it never
// reached the field read that is the actual verdict.
//
// The reference drops prelude declarations matched by KIND - struct for struct,
// enum for enum, trait for trait - and the impl only follows. Mirroring half of
// that left `str::from_slice` reaching for `.off` on a type that has no such
// field, and nothing said so.
struct Arena { x: int }

fn takes(a: *const Arena) -> int { return a.x; }

fn main() { let ar = Arena { x: 7 }; println(takes(&ar)); }
